      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To solve this problem, we need to determine the area of the square and the area of the circle and then find the difference between the two areas.\n\nFirst, let's find the side length of the square. Since the diagonal of the square is 5 inches, we can use the Pythagorean theorem to find the side length. The Pythagorean theorem states that in a right-angled triangle, the square of the hypotenuse is equal to the sum of the squares of the other two sides. In this case, the diagonal of the square acts as the hypotenuse.\n\nLet \\( s \\) be the length of one side of the square. According to the Pythagorean theorem:\n\\[\ns^2 + s^2 = 5^2\n\\]\n\\[\n2s^2 = 25\n\\]\n\\[\ns^2 = \\frac{25}{2}\n\\]\n\\[\ns = \\sqrt{\\frac{25}{2}}\n\\]\n\\[\ns = \\frac{5}{\\sqrt{2}} = \\frac{5}{\\sqrt{2}} \\times \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{2}} = \\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{2}\n\\]\n\nNow, we calculate the area of the square. The area \\( A_{\\text{square}} \\) of a square is given by:\n\\[\nA_{\\text{square}} = s^2\n\\]\nSubstituting the side length:\n\\[\nA_{\\text{square}} = \\left( \\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\right)^2 = \\frac{25 \\cdot 2}{4} = \\frac{50}{4} = 12.5 \\text{ square inches}\n\\]\n\nNext, let's find the area of the circle. The diameter of the circle is given as 5 inches, so the radius \\( r \\) is:\n\\[\nr = \\frac{5}{2} = 2.5 \\text{ inches}\n\\]\n\nThe area \\( A_{\\text{circle}} \\) of a circle is given by:\n\\[\nA_{\\text{circle}} = \\pi r^2\n\\]\nSubstituting the radius:\n\\[\nA_{\\text{circle}} = \\pi (2.5)^2 = \\pi \\cdot 6.25 \\approx 29.1 \\text{ square inches}\n\\]\n\nNow, we need to find the difference in area between the circle and the square:\n\\[\n\\Delta A = A_{\\text{circle}} - A_{\\text{square}}\n\\]\n\\[\n\\Delta A = 29.1 - 12.5 = 16.6 \\text{ square inches}\n\\]\n\nThus, the area of the circle is greater by \\(\\boxed{16.6}\\) square inches.",
